form of ''wenchel'', orig. a child, prob. < AS. *''wencel'', a child, represented by the once occurring ''winclo'', pl., children, prob. for *''wencelu'', neut. pl. of the adj. ''wencel'', ''wencele'', weak (found once, in dat. pl. ''wencelum'', applied to widows), var. of ''wancol'', ''woncol'', unstable, > E. ''wankle'': see ''wankle''. The AS. ''wencle'', a wench, a daughter, given by Somner, is an error based upon the above forms.] '''1†.''' A child (of either sex).
{{smaller block|Were & wif & ''wenchel'' [man and wife and child].
{{right|''Ancren Riwle'', p. 334.}}}}
'''2.''' A female child; a girl; a maid or damsel; a young woman in general. {{smaller|[''Wench'' had originally no depreciatory implication, and continued to be used in a respectful sense, especially as a familiar term, long after it had acquired such an implication in specific employment; and it is still commonly so used in provincial English, and sometimes archaically in literature.]}}
{{smaller block|William & his worthi ''wenche'' [a princess] than were blithe Of the help that thei hade of this wild best.
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1901.}}
Go ȝe awey, for the ''wenche'' is nat dead, but slepith.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Mat. ix. 24.}}
Now, how dost thou look now? O ill-starr’d ''wench'' [Desdemona]! {{right|''Shak.'', Othello, v. 2. 272.}}}}
'''3.''' Specifically—(''a'') A girl or young woman of a humble order or class; especially, a maid-servant; a working-girl.
{{smaller block|A ''wench'' [maid-servant, R. V.] went and told them.
{{right|2 Sam. xvii. 17.}}
The ''wench'' in the kitchen sings and scours from morning to night. {{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 248.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
wind: see ''wind''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To turn; change.
{{smaller block|To ''wenden'' thus here thoght.
{{right|''Genesis and Exodus'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 4061.}}}}
'''2.''' To direct (one’s way or course); proceed upon.
{{smaller block|''Wende'' forthe thi course, I comaunde the.
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 52.}}
<poem>And still, her thought that she was left alone
Uncompanied, great voyages to ''wend''
In desert land, her Tyrian folk to seek.</poem>
{{right|''Surrey'', Æneid, iv. 616.}}
<poem>Then slower ''wended'' back his way
Where the poor maiden bleeding lay.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., iv. 26.}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1†.''' To turn; make a turn; go round; veer.
{{smaller block|For so is this worlde ''went'' with hem that han powere.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), iii. 280.}}
<poem>At the ''wendyng'' [turning of the furrow] slake
The yoke, thyne oxen neckes forto cole.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 44.}}
The lesser [ship] will turn her broadsides twice before the greater can ''wend'' once. {{right|''Raleigh.''}}}}
'''2.''' To take one’s way or course; proceed; go.
{{smaller block|<poem>For every wyght which that to Rome ''went'' [wendeth]
Halt nat o path or alwey o manere.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 36.}}
<poem>As fer as any wight hath ever ''went''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, v. 444.}}
<poem>Hopeless and helpless doth Ægeon ''wend'',
But to procrastinate his lifeless end.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., i. 1. 158.}}
<poem>Bereft of thee he ''wends'' astray.</poem>
{{right|''Prior'', Wandering Pilgrim, st. 12.}}}}
'''3†.''' To pass away; disappear; depart; vanish.
